1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,664
This is ECE 320, video 12. We solve the pipeline design questions of two past midterms.

2
00:00:07,114 --> 00:00:11,294
After this video, you can add new instructions and new registers to the pipeline.

3
00:00:11,744 --> 00:00:13,927
You can judge a change to the branch hardware.

4
00:00:14,377 --> 00:00:19,346
And you can find the cost of a code pattern when M has a cache, and remove that cost.

5
00:00:19,796 --> 00:00:32,520
A design question asks you to change the pipeline. Use the same six questions every time. First, the new state. Which new registers hold the new values? In which stage are they read and written?

6
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:39,472
Second, the paths and the muxes. Which wires carry the new values? Which mux selects them?

7
00:00:39,922 --> 00:00:45,402
Third, the control signals. The decoder sets new signals for each new instruction.

8
00:00:45,852 --> 00:00:53,561
Fourth, the hazards. Find the new read after write cases. Then decide: forward by MX or WX, or stall.

9
00:00:54,011 --> 00:00:59,676
Fifth, precise state. Write new state in W, after M, the commit point.

10
00:01:00,126 --> 00:01:05,467
Sixth, run a program on the new hardware. Fill the pipeline diagram and count the cycles.

11
00:01:05,917 --> 00:01:12,233
A design question gives marks for each of these six parts. So name every new register, mux and signal.

12
00:01:12,683 --> 00:01:22,946
Question 4 of the fall 2019 midterm. Homer adds two 32-bit registers, HI and LO, in the decode stage. He also adds three new instructions.

13
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:26,740
W mul multiplies register rs1 by register rs2.

14
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:33,645
Each register has 32 bits. So the product has 32 plus 32, which is 64 bits.

15
00:01:34,095 --> 00:01:37,903
W mul writes the upper 32 bits of the product into HI.

16
00:01:38,353 --> 00:01:41,093
It writes the lower 32 bits into LO.

17
00:01:41,543 --> 00:01:45,304
Move from HI copies HI into register rd.

18
00:01:45,754 --> 00:01:49,145
Move from LO copies LO into register rd.

19
00:01:49,595 --> 00:01:55,818
The question asks for all the necessary changes to the 5-stage pipeline. Pause the video and try it.

20
00:01:56,268 --> 00:02:03,048
Start with one row for each instruction. W mul reads rs1 and rs2 in D, from the register file.

21
00:02:03,498 --> 00:02:06,656
In X, a multiplier makes the 64-bit product.

22
00:02:07,106 --> 00:02:13,374
We assume that the multiplier takes one X cycle. The question gives no latency, so state this in the exam.

23
00:02:13,824 --> 00:02:22,137
In M, the X M register carries the product. A class pipeline register holds a 32-bit result. So X M needs 32 more bits.

24
00:02:22,587 --> 00:02:32,897
In W, W mul writes HI and LO. The M W register also carries all 64 bits, so it also gets 32 more bits.

25
00:02:33,347 --> 00:02:41,474
Move from HI and move from LO read HI or LO in D. A new mux selects the value.

26
00:02:41,924 --> 00:02:45,500
In X, the ALU passes the value to its output.

27
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:53,088
In W, the instruction writes rd, as any ALU instruction does. Reg W En is 1.

28
00:02:53,538 --> 00:02:59,065
So treat HI and LO like the register file. Read them in D, and write them in W.

29
00:02:59,515 --> 00:03:11,728
Do not write HI and LO at the end of X. An older instruction can still raise an exception in M. The handler then sees a result of a younger instruction. That state is not precise.

30
00:03:12,178 --> 00:03:17,612
W comes after M, the commit point. So a write in W keeps the state precise.

31
00:03:18,062 --> 00:03:25,910
The figure shows the class pipeline with forwarding, in gray. It has the five stages F, D, X, M and W.

32
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:32,444
The green line is the MX path. It carries a result from the X M register to the ALU input A.

33
00:03:32,894 --> 00:03:38,029
The blue line is the WX path. It carries a result from W to the ALU input A.

34
00:03:38,479 --> 00:03:42,984
Change one. Add the two new registers, HI and LO, in D.

35
00:03:43,434 --> 00:03:54,765
Change two. Add a mux in D, before the D X register. Its select signal, H L Sel, picks rs1, HI or LO for the ALU input A.

36
00:03:55,215 --> 00:04:00,602
Change three. Extend the ALU with a multiplier. Its result has 64 bits.

37
00:04:01,052 --> 00:04:09,086
A class pipeline register holds a 32-bit result. So the X M and M W registers each grow from 32 to 64 bits.

38
00:04:09,536 --> 00:04:16,595
Change four. In W, bits 63 to 32 of the M W register go to HI.

39
00:04:17,045 --> 00:04:19,181
Bits 31 to 0 go to LO.

40
00:04:19,631 --> 00:04:25,994
A new write enable, H L W En, controls this write. It is 1 only for W mul.

41
00:04:26,444 --> 00:04:31,784
Change five. The forwarding mux of input A must select the correct half of a forwarded product.

42
00:04:32,234 --> 00:04:34,231
Move from LO takes the lower half.

43
00:04:34,681 --> 00:04:36,771
Move from HI takes the upper half.

44
00:04:37,221 --> 00:04:44,791
Now the decoder. W mul sets H L Sel to rs1. So the ALU input A gets the rs1 value.

45
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:48,352
A L U Sel selects multiply.

46
00:04:48,802 --> 00:04:52,424
H L W En is 1, so W writes HI and LO.

47
00:04:52,874 --> 00:04:57,793
Reg W En is 0, because W mul writes no x register.

48
00:04:58,243 --> 00:05:01,540
Move from HI sets H L Sel to HI.

49
00:05:01,990 --> 00:05:08,445
A L U Sel is pass A. The ALU passes input A to its output.

50
00:05:08,895 --> 00:05:11,589
Reg W En is 1, so W writes rd.

51
00:05:12,039 --> 00:05:14,871
W B Sel selects the ALU result.

52
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:19,269
Move from LO is the same, but H L Sel selects LO.

53
00:05:19,719 --> 00:05:23,063
All other instructions set H L Sel to rs1.

54
00:05:23,513 --> 00:05:28,900
They set H L W En to 0, so they never write HI or LO.

55
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:31,300
Their other signals do not change.

56
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:37,369
The names H L Sel and H L W En are our names. The question gives no names.

57
00:05:37,819 --> 00:05:44,274
Next, the hazards. Only W mul writes HI and LO, and it writes them in W.

58
00:05:44,724 --> 00:05:51,598
Move from HI and move from LO read HI or LO in D. They use the value in X.

59
00:05:52,048 --> 00:05:56,738
So the forwarding unit compares opcodes. It does not need to compare register numbers.

60
00:05:57,188 --> 00:06:03,922
Case one, MX. In this short run, move from HI, number 2, is in X in cycle 4.

61
00:06:04,372 --> 00:06:10,270
W mul, number 1, is in M in the same cycle. The X M register holds the new HI.

62
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:16,060
So mux A selects the X M register. This is the MX condition.

63
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:23,151
Case two, WX. In this run, move from LO, number 3, is in X in cycle 5.

64
00:06:23,601 --> 00:06:26,062
W mul is in W in the same cycle.

65
00:06:26,512 --> 00:06:33,402
The instruction in M, number 2, is not a W mul. A W mul in M would hold a newer value.

66
00:06:33,852 --> 00:06:38,357
So mux A selects the M W register. This is the WX condition.

67
00:06:38,807 --> 00:06:46,005
Case three. In this run, move from HI, number 4, is in D in cycle 5.

68
00:06:46,455 --> 00:06:53,096
W mul is in W in the same cycle. There is no forwarding into D, so D reads the old HI.

69
00:06:53,546 --> 00:06:58,793
So number 4 stalls one cycle: D star. This is the W D case.

70
00:06:59,243 --> 00:07:10,201
The x registers need no change. W mul reads rs1 and rs2, and the move instructions write rd. The class forwarding and stall logic handles these.

71
00:07:10,651 --> 00:07:16,967
Part B asks you to run this sequence on the new pipeline. Use arrows for forwarding. Pause the video and try it.

72
00:07:17,417 --> 00:07:26,380
Number 1, W mul, multiplies in X in cycle 3. We assume one X cycle, so the product exists at the end of cycle 3.

73
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:30,824
Number 2, move from LO, is in X in cycle 4.

74
00:07:31,274 --> 00:07:37,636
W mul is in M in cycle 4. So LO goes by MX, from the X M register.

75
00:07:38,086 --> 00:07:41,708
Number 3, move from HI, is in X in cycle 5.

76
00:07:42,158 --> 00:07:48,428
W mul is in W in cycle 5. So HI goes by WX, from the M W register.

77
00:07:48,878 --> 00:07:52,964
Number 4, move from LO, is in D in cycle 5.

78
00:07:53,414 --> 00:08:03,445
W mul writes LO in W in the same cycle. There is no forwarding into D. So D reads the old LO, and number 4 repeats D: D star.

79
00:08:03,895 --> 00:08:08,261
Number 4 holds D, so number 5 repeats F: F star.

80
00:08:08,711 --> 00:08:12,380
In cycle 6, number 4 reads the new LO in D.

81
00:08:12,830 --> 00:08:17,799
In cycle 7, the store, number 5, reads x 6 in D.

82
00:08:18,249 --> 00:08:24,193
Number 2 wrote x 6 in W in cycle 6. So the register file already has it.

83
00:08:24,643 --> 00:08:26,361
The sequence takes 10 cycles.

84
00:08:26,811 --> 00:08:31,502
There is one stall cycle in D and one in F, both in cycle 5.

85
00:08:31,952 --> 00:08:39,289
Forwarding table, row 1. Number 1 forwards LO to number 2, from M to X, in cycle 4.

86
00:08:39,739 --> 00:08:45,916
Row 2. Number 1 forwards HI to number 3, from W to X, in cycle 5.

87
00:08:46,366 --> 00:08:57,047
Stall table, row 1. Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 5. W mul writes LO in W in the same cycle, so D reads the old LO.

88
00:08:57,497 --> 00:09:03,023
Row 2. Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 5, because number 4 holds D.

89
00:09:03,473 --> 00:09:11,600
This answer uses the model design of Part A, with a one-cycle multiply. With a slower multiplier, the cycles change. So state your assumption.

90
00:09:12,050 --> 00:09:19,899
Part C. Homer removes the branch compare unit from X. The ALU compares instead, and a new adder computes the target.

91
00:09:20,349 --> 00:09:27,036
The figure shows the X stage of the class pipeline. There, the ALU computes the branch target, PC plus immediate.

92
00:09:27,486 --> 00:09:32,223
The branch compare unit takes its inputs from the D X register, before the forwarding muxes.

93
00:09:32,673 --> 00:09:39,394
So the compare unit gets no forwarded values. The branch must read its operands in D, after the W of the producer.

94
00:09:39,844 --> 00:09:46,903
In Homer's design, the ALU compares the two operands. It gives B R equal and B R less than.

95
00:09:47,353 --> 00:09:54,923
The ALU inputs come after the forwarding muxes. So the branch gets its operands by MX or WX, with no new paths.

96
00:09:55,373 --> 00:10:02,200
The ALU is now busy with the compare. So a new adder computes the target, PC plus immediate, at the same time.

97
00:10:02,650 --> 00:10:04,832
The branch still resolves in X.

98
00:10:05,282 --> 00:10:09,230
Pause the video and try it. Find how many stall cycles Homer saves.

99
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:15,113
First, the class pipeline. The branch, number 2, comes right after add, which writes x 1.

100
00:10:15,563 --> 00:10:18,721
Add writes x 1 in W, in cycle 5.

101
00:10:19,171 --> 00:10:25,023
The branch must read x 1 in D after that W. So it reads x 1 in cycle 6.

102
00:10:25,473 --> 00:10:31,185
Before that, the branch repeats D in cycles 3, 4 and 5. That is 3 stall cycles.

103
00:10:31,635 --> 00:10:34,932
In Homer's design, the branch is in X in cycle 4.

104
00:10:35,382 --> 00:10:40,630
Add is in M in cycle 4. So x 1 goes by MX, and there is no stall.

105
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:43,762
The run takes 8 cycles, not 11.

106
00:10:44,212 --> 00:10:48,717
With a load as the producer, the class pipeline also stalls 3 cycles.

107
00:10:49,167 --> 00:10:52,836
Homer's design stalls one cycle: the load-use case.

108
00:10:53,286 --> 00:10:56,769
Then x 1 goes by WX, in cycle 5.

109
00:10:57,219 --> 00:10:59,727
The run takes 9 cycles, not 11.

110
00:11:00,177 --> 00:11:04,495
Now a taken branch in Homer's design. It resolves in X, in cycle 4.

111
00:11:04,945 --> 00:11:08,336
The two younger instructions are flushed. They show dashes.

112
00:11:08,786 --> 00:11:17,005
The target is fetched in cycle 5, not 3. So a wrong guess still costs 2 cycles: the fetches in cycles 3 and 4.

113
00:11:17,455 --> 00:11:25,118
The answer for Part C: Homer removes the stalls of a branch that uses a recent result. The penalty of a wrong guess does not change.

114
00:11:25,568 --> 00:11:30,630
Now question 4 of the fall 2022 paper. It uses the class pipeline with forwarding.

115
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:33,030
There is no forwarding into D.

116
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:36,406
There is also no forwarding into the branch compare unit.

117
00:11:36,856 --> 00:11:42,893
A C L S D pattern is a sequence of consecutive loads that write the same destination register.

118
00:11:43,343 --> 00:11:48,730
In program P, numbers 1 and 2 both load into x 1. This is a write after write pair.

119
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:51,920
Nobody reads the x 1 value of number 1.

120
00:11:52,370 --> 00:11:57,200
Number 4 reads x 1. Its producer is number 2, the latest writer of x 1.

121
00:11:57,650 --> 00:12:03,734
Part A asks you to execute program P and explain every stall. Pause the video and try it.

122
00:12:04,184 --> 00:12:12,404
The instructions enter the pipeline one per cycle. Number 1 enters in cycle 1, number 2 in cycle 2, number 3 in cycle 3.

123
00:12:12,854 --> 00:12:18,380
Number 1 computes its address, x 4 plus 0, in X in cycle 3.

124
00:12:18,830 --> 00:12:25,796
Number 1 reads D mem in M, in cycle 4. Its value exists at the end of cycle 4.

125
00:12:26,246 --> 00:12:33,165
Number 3 reads x 3 in D, in cycle 4. No instruction before it writes x 3, so there is no hazard.

126
00:12:33,615 --> 00:12:36,495
Number 4 is in X in cycle 6.

127
00:12:36,945 --> 00:12:44,561
Number 2, the latest writer of x 1, is in W in cycle 6. So x 1 goes by WX. There is no stall.

128
00:12:45,011 --> 00:12:51,231
Program P takes 8 cycles with no stalls. So the stall explanation is: there are none.

129
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:55,814
The work of number 1 is wasted, because number 2 overwrites x 1.

130
00:12:56,264 --> 00:13:01,604
Part B adds a cache in M. The data memory is a 2-way set associative cache.

131
00:13:02,054 --> 00:13:03,726
It has 16 sets.

132
00:13:04,176 --> 00:13:06,127
Each block has 16 bytes.

133
00:13:06,577 --> 00:13:13,032
It uses L R U replacement. A larger age means that the block was used less recently.

134
00:13:13,482 --> 00:13:16,268
M stalls on a miss. A hit takes 1 cycle.

135
00:13:16,718 --> 00:13:19,830
A miss with a vacant way takes 2 cycles.

136
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:24,366
A miss in a full set evicts a block and takes 4 cycles.

137
00:13:24,816 --> 00:13:29,321
The table shows the cache at the start: sets 0 to 3, each with two ways.

138
00:13:29,771 --> 00:13:33,765
At the start of program P, x 4 holds hex 410.

139
00:13:34,215 --> 00:13:36,537
And x 5 holds hex 610.

140
00:13:36,987 --> 00:13:46,275
Part B asks for the pipeline diagram, the final registers and cache, and the M latency of numbers 1 and 2. Pause the video and try it.

141
00:13:46,725 --> 00:13:54,852
First, split each address. A block has 16 bytes, and 16 is 2 to the 4. So the offset has 4 bits.

142
00:13:55,302 --> 00:14:01,432
The cache has 16 sets, and 16 is 2 to the 4. So the index has 4 bits.

143
00:14:01,882 --> 00:14:07,687
The tag is the rest: 32 minus 4 minus 4, which is 24 bits.

144
00:14:08,137 --> 00:14:18,865
Number 1 loads from hex 410. Each hex digit gives 4 bits: 0 1 0 0, then 0 0 0 1, then 0 0 0 0.

145
00:14:19,315 --> 00:14:22,751
The 4 offset bits are all 0, so the offset is 0.

146
00:14:23,201 --> 00:14:28,635
The 4 index bits are 0 0 0 1, so number 1 uses set 1.

147
00:14:29,085 --> 00:14:34,239
The tag bits are 0 1 0 0, with zeros above them. So the tag is hex 4.

148
00:14:34,689 --> 00:14:44,117
Number 2 loads from hex 610. Its bits are 0 1 1 0, then 0 0 0 1, then 0 0 0 0. Its offset is also 0.

149
00:14:44,567 --> 00:14:50,325
Its index bits are also 0 0 0 1, so number 2 also uses set 1.

150
00:14:50,775 --> 00:14:55,744
Its tag bits are 0 1 1 0, so the tag is hex 6.

151
00:14:56,194 --> 00:14:58,145
So both loads use set 1.

152
00:14:58,595 --> 00:15:02,589
Set 1 of the given cache holds tag hex 6 in way 0.

153
00:15:03,039 --> 00:15:05,314
Way 1 holds tag hex 8.

154
00:15:05,764 --> 00:15:11,894
Number 2 has tag hex 6. So the block of number 2 is already in the cache, in way 0.

155
00:15:12,344 --> 00:15:20,657
Now run the two loads through set 1. At the start, way 0 has age 1. So way 0 is the least recently used block.

156
00:15:21,107 --> 00:15:31,138
Number 1 looks for tag hex 4. Way 0 holds tag hex 6, and way 1 holds tag hex 8. No way matches, so it is a miss.

157
00:15:31,588 --> 00:15:39,018
Both ways are valid, so the set is full. L R U picks way 0, the larger age, and evicts tag hex 6.

158
00:15:39,468 --> 00:15:42,162
A miss in a full set takes 4 cycles.

159
00:15:42,612 --> 00:15:48,925
Then the ages change. The used way, way 0, gets age 0.

160
00:15:49,375 --> 00:15:56,573
Way 1 had age 0, lower than the old age of way 0, which was 1. So way 1 gets plus 1: age 1.

161
00:15:57,023 --> 00:16:04,314
Number 2 looks for tag hex 6. The set now holds tags hex 4 and hex 8. So this is a miss too.

162
00:16:04,764 --> 00:16:10,244
Way 1 now has the larger age, 1. So L R U evicts way 1, tag hex 8.

163
00:16:10,694 --> 00:16:16,964
The ages change again. Way 1 gets age 0, and way 0 gets age 1.

164
00:16:17,414 --> 00:16:19,828
The latency is again 4 cycles.

165
00:16:20,278 --> 00:16:26,409
This is the important point. The dead load, number 1, evicted the block that number 2 needs.

166
00:16:26,859 --> 00:16:32,617
Number 1 enters M in cycle 4. It misses in a full set, so M takes 4 cycles.

167
00:16:33,067 --> 00:16:38,640
M is busy, so number 2 cannot enter M. It repeats X: X star.

168
00:16:39,090 --> 00:16:46,056
Number 1 stays in M in cycles 4, 5, 6 and 7. Every repeat except the last gets a star.

169
00:16:46,506 --> 00:16:50,407
Number 3 repeats D in cycles 4, 5 and 6.

170
00:16:50,857 --> 00:16:53,225
Number 4 repeats F in the same cycles.

171
00:16:53,675 --> 00:16:57,344
Number 1 writes x 1 in W, in cycle 8.

172
00:16:57,794 --> 00:17:03,970
Number 2 enters M in cycle 8. It also misses in a full set, so it stays in M for cycles 8 to 11.

173
00:17:04,420 --> 00:17:08,043
Number 2 writes x 1 in W in cycle 12.

174
00:17:08,493 --> 00:17:15,319
Number 4 is in X in cycle 12. So x 1 goes by WX, with no extra stall.

175
00:17:15,769 --> 00:17:18,402
Program P now takes 14 cycles.

176
00:17:18,852 --> 00:17:23,914
Part A took 8 cycles. So the misses add 14 minus 8, which is 6 cycles.

177
00:17:24,364 --> 00:17:28,776
The M latency is 4 cycles for number 1, and 4 cycles for number 2.

178
00:17:29,226 --> 00:17:36,238
The final registers. x 1 holds the word at address hex 610, the value that number 2 loads.

179
00:17:36,688 --> 00:17:43,933
x 3 was hex 2 F 0. Number 3 adds 1, so x 3 is hex 2 F 1.

180
00:17:44,383 --> 00:17:49,630
Number 4 adds 1 to x 1. So x 6 is that memory word plus 1.

181
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:52,031
The other registers do not change.

182
00:17:52,481 --> 00:17:55,267
The final cache. Only set 1 changes.

183
00:17:55,717 --> 00:17:59,757
In set 1, way 0 holds tag hex 4 with age 1.

184
00:18:00,207 --> 00:18:03,365
Way 1 holds tag hex 6 with age 0.

185
00:18:03,815 --> 00:18:08,041
The M latency of number 1 is 4 cycles: a miss in a full set.

186
00:18:08,491 --> 00:18:13,275
Number 2 also takes 4 cycles. It misses, because number 1 evicted its block.

187
00:18:13,725 --> 00:18:21,666
The exam gives no memory values. So write x 1 as the memory word at hex 610, and x 6 as that word plus 1.

188
00:18:22,116 --> 00:18:29,407
Part C asks for the side effects. If every access hits, program P takes 8 cycles, as in Part A.

189
00:18:29,857 --> 00:18:34,965
Side effect one. Number 1 misses: 4 cycles in M, 3 more than a 1-cycle hit.

190
00:18:35,415 --> 00:18:41,824
Nobody reads the x 1 value of number 1. So M is busy for a dead value. This degrades performance.

191
00:18:42,274 --> 00:18:49,054
Side effect two. Number 1 changes the cache. It evicts block hex 6, the block that number 2 needs.

192
00:18:49,504 --> 00:18:55,263
So number 2 misses too, and adds 3 more cycles. This also degrades performance.

193
00:18:55,713 --> 00:19:01,007
In total, 8 plus 3 plus 3 is 14 cycles, the result of Part B.

194
00:19:01,457 --> 00:19:10,931
Side effect three. In another program, the block of number 1 can be useful. A later load to that block then hits. This benefits performance.

195
00:19:11,381 --> 00:19:16,721
Without the access of number 1, number 2 hits, because block hex 6 stays in the cache.

196
00:19:17,171 --> 00:19:20,419
Program P then takes 8 cycles, not 14.

197
00:19:20,869 --> 00:19:25,421
Part D asks for a change to the control. Look at cycle 3.

198
00:19:25,871 --> 00:19:30,282
In cycle 3, number 2 is in D. The F D register holds it.

199
00:19:30,732 --> 00:19:33,983
Number 1 is in X. The D X register holds it.

200
00:19:34,433 --> 00:19:38,984
The class pipeline already has a kill or no op mux before D X, for stalls.

201
00:19:39,434 --> 00:19:44,125
Add a new check to the hazard unit. First, the instruction in X is a load.

202
00:19:44,575 --> 00:19:47,268
Second, the instruction in D is a load.

203
00:19:47,718 --> 00:19:49,808
Third, both have the same rd.

204
00:19:50,258 --> 00:19:52,952
Fourth, the load in D does not read that rd.

205
00:19:53,402 --> 00:20:00,275
Add a new mux before the X M register. Its second input is a no op: an instruction that does nothing.

206
00:20:00,725 --> 00:20:06,948
If all four checks are true, the hazard unit sets a kill signal. Then the new mux selects the no op.

207
00:20:07,398 --> 00:20:11,577
In cycle 4, the X M register holds the no op, not number 1.

208
00:20:12,027 --> 00:20:18,900
The no op does not access the cache, and it writes no register in W. So number 1 shows dashes in M and W.

209
00:20:19,350 --> 00:20:29,312
Do not kill number 1 if number 2 reads rd. For example, load word x 1, 0 of x 1 needs the x 1 value of number 1.

210
00:20:29,762 --> 00:20:38,024
One more caution. If number 1 had a bad address, it would raise an exception. The kill removes that exception. Mention this in the exam.

211
00:20:38,474 --> 00:20:45,858
Now run program P with the kill. In cycle 3, number 1 is in X and number 2 is in D. Both are loads to x 1.

212
00:20:46,308 --> 00:20:50,394
Number 2 does not read x 1. So the hazard unit kills number 1.

213
00:20:50,844 --> 00:20:55,024
Number 1 shows dashes in M and W. It does not access the cache.

214
00:20:55,474 --> 00:20:59,096
Number 2 accesses set 1 in M, in cycle 5.

215
00:20:59,546 --> 00:21:03,447
Its tag, hex 6, is still in way 0. So this is a hit.

216
00:21:03,897 --> 00:21:05,986
A hit takes 1 cycle in M.

217
00:21:06,436 --> 00:21:10,151
Then way 0 gets age 0, and way 1 gets age 1.

218
00:21:10,601 --> 00:21:15,663
Number 4 gets x 1 from number 2 by WX, in cycle 6, as in Part A.

219
00:21:16,113 --> 00:21:24,324
Program P takes 8 cycles. Part B took 14 cycles. So the kill saves 14 minus 8, which is 6 cycles.

220
00:21:24,774 --> 00:21:31,657
The registers end with the same values. So yes, changes to the pipeline control can improve the C L S D pattern.

221
00:21:32,107 --> 00:21:42,742
The recap. One. Answer a design question with a list: new state, paths and muxes, control signals, hazards, precise state, and a run.

222
00:21:43,192 --> 00:21:54,307
Two. HI and LO are read in D and written in W. Add a mux in D and a multiplier in X. Widen X M and M W by 32 bits.

223
00:21:54,757 --> 00:22:06,367
Three. A read of HI or LO uses MX, WX, or a one-cycle W D stall. The check compares opcodes, because only W mul writes HI and LO.

224
00:22:06,817 --> 00:22:13,922
Four. With the compare in the ALU, a branch gets forwarded values. Three stalls become zero, or one after a load.

225
00:22:14,372 --> 00:22:20,084
Five. The wrong-guess penalty stays 2 cycles, because the branch still resolves in X.

226
00:22:20,534 --> 00:22:27,546
Six. In the C L S D pattern, the dead load misses and evicts the block of the next load. Program P takes 14 cycles.

227
00:22:27,996 --> 00:22:37,749
Seven. The fix: detect two loads to the same rd in D and X, and turn the older load into a no op. That is the end of video 12.
